      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "808dec29-0dbb-5384-aca9-76f5077024dd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $({y}^{‴})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$\n- **II.** $({y}^{′})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$\n- **III.** $({y}^{‴})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is also a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c8f51d56-c42e-5c60-8b58-cece75eefc31",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
