    "question": {
      ":question/id": "00550628-cf53-5ef2-8d47-f562eb0aaa62",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\sqrt{{y}^{″}+x}=3{y}^{4}$\n- **II.** $({y}^{′})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$\n- **III.** $({y}^{′})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
