      ":question/id": "d09f68ae-8279-5e60-b154-b7d446a473dc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $({y}^{″})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **III.** $({y}^{‴})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
